    THIS FILE'S ROLE IN THE OVERALL POC
    ===================================
    This report is the at-a-glance status distribution donut chart shown
    on the Agent Workspace dashboard
    (../dashboards/pa_dashboards_x_casemgmt_agent_workspace.xml). It sits
    alongside two list widgets ("My Open Cases" and "My Overdue Tasks")
    to give an agent a complete operational view at login:

      - "My Open Cases" list (../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml)
        shows the agent's PERSONAL queue of actionable cases.
      - "My Overdue Tasks" list (../reports/x_casemgmt_my_overdue_tasks.xml)
        shows the agent's PERSONAL list of late tasks.
      - THIS donut shows the status distribution of the VIEWING USER's own
        case portfolio. The agent uses it to see the shape of their own
        backlog at a glance - how much of it is still Draft or Open, how
        much is Pending on someone else, how much is already Resolved -
        without leaving the dashboard.

    All three widgets on this dashboard are therefore filtered per-viewer.

    WHY THIS REPORT IS FILTERED (QA finding F5)
    ===========================================
    An earlier revision shipped this report with an EMPTY filter and
    described it as "UNFILTERED by design - the entire team backlog". That
    was measured to be a disclosure: report execution does NOT apply the
    row-level read ACL that governs the case list, so under the
    x_casemgmt_demo_agent persona the donut aggregated EVERY case on the
    instance and disclosed the status distribution of records the agent is
    refused on every other surface (form load answers "Security
    constraints prevent access to requested page", the Table API answers
    404). Counts only - no subject, number or other record content leaked -
    but the count of cases in each lifecycle stage is exactly the
    information an "Assigned only" grant withholds.

    The AAP contains a genuine tension here and it is resolved in favour of
    the normative requirements:
      - Section 0.5.1's transformation table describes this row as a
        "Donut grouped by status" and says nothing about a filter, which is
        how the empty filter was originally justified.
      - Section 0.5.6 confines case_agent to "Read - Assigned only", and
        Section 0.7.3 Gate 3 states the criterion verbatim: "case_agent
        cannot access unassigned cases".
      - Section 0.4.4 places THIS widget on the AGENT WORKSPACE, beside
        "My open cases" and "My overdue tasks" - a dashboard whose whole
        purpose is the viewer's own work, and whose other two widgets both
        filter on javascript:gs.getUserID().
    A descriptive sentence in the transformation table cannot widen an
    access-control mandate, so the filter below scopes the aggregate to the
    viewing user. The portfolio-wide view the AAP does call for is
    unaffected: Section 0.4.4's Manager View keeps the deliberately
    unfiltered "All Cases by Status" bar chart
    (../reports/x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_status.xml), which is bound to a
    dashboard shared only with x_casemgmt_case_manager - the role Section
    0.5.6 grants "Read - All". The report's title stays exactly as Section
    0.4.4 names it, "Case Count by Status".

    The filter mirrors the three limbs of the agent read ACL
    (../acl/x_casemgmt_case_read_agent_assigned.xml) so the donut totals
    exactly what the viewer can open:
        assigned_agent=javascript:gs.getUserID()
        ^ORassigned_groupINjavascript:gs.getUser().getMyGroups()
        ^ORsys_created_by=javascript:gs.getUserName()
    Every limb is a single expression, which is a hard requirement of the
    encoded-query evaluator: measured on this instance, a javascript: value
    written as an IIFE or as a multi-statement body evaluates to EMPTY, and
    an empty value on the `=` operator silently degrades to "field is
    empty" - so a filter built that way would have quietly answered the
    wrong question. No hard-coded sys_id appears: the user, their groups
    and their user_name are all resolved at execution time.

    Consequence for the manager, stated plainly: the Agent Workspace is
    shared with x_casemgmt_case_manager as well, and a manager holding no
    assignments now sees an empty donut on THIS dashboard - the same
    outcome its two sibling "My ..." widgets already produce for that
    persona, and the reason Section 0.4.4 gives the manager a separate
    Manager View with the unfiltered aggregates.

    SEMANTIC SOURCE-SIDE ANCHOR (READ-ONLY, NOT BYTE-FOR-BYTE PORTED)
    =================================================================
    The semantic ancestors of this report in the existing ArkCase
    codebase are three Java artifacts in the case-file-plugin:

      - acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-case-file-plugin/src/main/
        java/com/armedia/acm/plugins/casefile/model/CaseFile.java
        Lines 141-142:
            @Column(name = "cm_case_status")
            private String status;
        ArkCase tracks case status as a free-form String column on the
        `acm_case_file` MySQL table. The ServiceNow target normalizes
        this to a Choice field with exactly six values per AAP Section
        0.5.7 (Draft, Open, In Progress, Pending, Resolved, Closed)
        defined in ../choices/sys_choice_case_status.xml. The
        <field>status</field> below references this Choice field
        by element name; the donut renders one slice per Choice label.

      - acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-case-file-plugin/src/main/
        java/com/armedia/acm/plugins/casefile/model/CaseByStatusDto.java
        Lines 33-57:
            public class CaseByStatusDto {
                private String status;
                private int    count;
                ... getters and setters ...
            }
        The DTO that ArkCase produced when aggregating cases by status -
        a (status, count) tuple consumed by Pentaho-backed reports. The
        ServiceNow target replaces this DTO + Pentaho pipeline with a
        native sys_report whose `<field>status</field>` plus
        `<aggregate>COUNT</aggregate>` produce the same tuple shape
        directly via the platform's GlideAggregate engine - no DTO,
        no external BI tool, no Solr facet. The dashboard widget renders
        the (status, count) pairs as donut slices.

      - acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-case-file-plugin/src/main/
        java/com/armedia/acm/plugins/casefile/state/AcmCasesState.java
        Lines 32-45:
            public class AcmCasesState extends StateOfModule {
                private Long numberOfCases;
                ...
            }
        ArkCase exposed a single-cell case-count via this state-carrier;
        the ServiceNow equivalent at THIS report's level is the SUM of
        all donut slices (i.e., total count across the six statuses).
        The dashboard widget can render that total in the chart center
        based on the aggregated COUNT - a UX convention handled by the
        widget rendering layer, not THIS report definition.

      - acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-case-file-plugin/src/main/
        java/com/armedia/acm/plugins/casefile/model/
        CaseSummaryByStatusAndTimePeriodDto.java:
            private String                  timePeriod;
            private List<CaseByStatusDto>   caseByStatusDtoList;
        ArkCase aggregated cases by status across time periods using
        a custom DTO consumed by Pentaho BI reports. The aggregator was
        Pentaho-side, NOT JPA-side. The ServiceNow target replaces
        Pentaho-driven time-period aggregation with platform-native
        sys_report records (this file plus the seven sibling reports
        in this folder), all powered by ServiceNow's native GlideAggregate
        engine instead of an external BI engine - per AAP Section 0.1.2
        ("Reporting transformation: Pentaho BI + Solr aggregates ->
        ServiceNow Reports + Performance Analytics-style dashboards").

    DESIGN DECISIONS LOCKED IN BY THIS FILE
    =======================================
      - Aggregation discipline (AAP Section 0.5.1, this report's row):
        type = pie. Per agent-prompt Mandatory Field Specification,
        chart-type reports populate <type>, <field>, and <aggregate>;
        they leave <field_list/> and <aggregation_source/> empty. The
        rendered output is one donut/pie chart with one slice per
        distinct value of `status`.

      - Group-by discipline (agent-prompt Rule 4 verbatim):
        <field>status</field>. The Choice field defined in
        ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_status.xml. The six slices
        correspond to the six Choice values defined in
        ../choices/sys_choice_case_status.xml: Draft, Open, In Progress,
        Pending, Resolved, Closed (Title Case match required).

      - COLUMN NAME OF THE GROUP-BY (measured, not assumed): the
        group-by dimension is carried in the `field` column, whose
        label is "Field Name". `sys_report` has NO `group_by` column
        on this release - sys_dictionary for sys_report offers
        `field`, `additional_groupby`, `group` (a reference to
        sys_user_group), `sc_groupby_item_id` and
        `sc_groupby_variable_id`, and nothing named `group_by`. An
        earlier revision of this file emitted <group_by>status</group_by>;
        the loader silently discarded the unknown element, `field`
        stayed empty, and the Report renderer then fell back to the
        alphabetically-first field on the table (Assigned Agent), so
        the chart drew a plausible-looking but semantically WRONG
        dimension with no error and no empty state. The element is
        now <field>status</field>, matching the sibling
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml, which always
        rendered correctly precisely because it named its column in
        `field`. The `group_by` element has been removed outright
        rather than left empty: it is not a column, so emitting it -
        even empty - only invites the next reader to believe it is one.
        The same applies to the `format` element the earlier revision
        emitted; sys_report has no `format` column either, and both are
        now gone from all eight report artifacts.

      - Filter discipline: <filter> carries the three-limb per-viewer
        predicate described under "WHY THIS REPORT IS FILTERED" above.
        It counts the cases the viewing user may actually read - the ones
        assigned to them, the ones assigned to a group they belong to, and
        the ones they created - and nothing else. Type and priority are
        not filtered: the donut groups by status across the viewer's whole
        portfolio, including terminal Closed cases, which is what makes it
        complementary to the "My Open Cases" list report
        (../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml), whose own filter
        additionally excludes Resolved and Closed.

      - Aggregation discipline (agent-prompt Rule 6 verbatim):
        <aggregate>COUNT</aggregate>. Counts records per status bucket.
        <aggregation_source/> empty (COUNT does not require a numeric
        source field; it counts rows, not values).

      - Field-list discipline (agent-prompt Rule 9 verbatim):
        <field_list/> empty. Chart reports do NOT specify a field list;
        the field list is meaningful only for `type=list` reports per
        the sibling pattern in ../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml
        (which uses <field_list>number,subject,priority,status,opened_date</field_list>).
        For chart reports, the chart-type and group-by fully determine
        what is rendered.

      - Per-viewer semantics: this report resolves the viewer's identity
        at execution time (gs.getUserID(), gs.getUser().getMyGroups() and
        gs.getUserName() in the filter), so each viewer gets their own
        aggregate. Therefore:
          1) The aggregate is computed per user rather than once per
             cache cycle. <is_real_time>false</is_real_time> below still
             permits the platform's normal caching of a single viewer's
             result; the cache key includes the evaluated filter, so no
             viewer is ever served another viewer's numbers.
          2) Roles no longer see the same numbers, and that is the point.
             A `case_agent` sees exactly the portfolio its read ACL
             permits (measured: the six-status distribution of its own
             cases, not the instance-wide distribution). A `case_manager`
             sees its own assignments here and the full portfolio on the
             Manager View's unfiltered bar chart. A `case_viewer` is not
             bound to either dashboard (AAP Section 0.4.4 names only
             manager and agent), so it never renders this widget at all.

      - Why "type=donut", corrected from "pie": AAP Section 0.4.4 and
        Section 0.5.1 both specify a DONUT chart here, and `donut` IS a
        valid sys_report.type value on this release. Earlier revisions of
        this artifact shipped `pie` and justified it with two claims that
        have since been MEASURED FALSE, so they are recorded here rather
        than silently deleted:
          (1) "sys_report.type has no literal donut enum" - it does. The
              active sys_choice list for sys_report.type contains `donut`
              (label "Donut") and `semi_donut` ("Semi donut") under the
              platform's own "Pies and Donuts" group header, alongside
              `pie`, and four out-of-box reports on this instance already
              use it.
          (2) "the platform renders a pie with a centre hole once the
              report is embedded in a Dashboard widget" - it does not.
              Rendered as `pie`, the Highcharts point path terminated
              `A 0 0 0 0 1 ...` (inner radius 0) and the widget painted a
              SOLID pie with no hole. The report's own type is what
              decides it; there is no widget-level promotion.
        The distinction is therefore a report-level setting, and matching
        the AAP takes precedence over the aesthetic argument the old
        comment made. Cross-version note per AAP Section 0.4.2
        ("Yokohama-and-later feature floor"): `donut` is not new - it is
        long-standing sys_report vocabulary, so this does not narrow the
        release floor.

      - Why both this donut AND a parallel bar chart of the same
        aggregation: per AAP Section 0.4.4 the Manager View dashboard
        carries a separate "All Cases by Status" bar chart
        (../reports/x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_status.xml). Both reports
        share identical <table>, <field> and <aggregate>; they differ in
        <type> (donut vs. bar), in which dashboard consumes them (Agent
        Workspace vs. Manager View) and - since QA finding F5 - in
        <filter>: THIS report is scoped to the viewing user, while the
        Manager View bar chart stays deliberately unfiltered because its
        dashboard is shared only with x_casemgmt_case_manager, the role
        AAP Section 0.5.6 grants "Read - All". The duplication is
        INTENTIONAL per AAP Section 0.4.4 to give each audience the
        visualization optimized for their decision-making style, and the
        filter split is what keeps each audience inside its own grant:
          - Agents: at-a-glance proportional view (donut) of THEIR OWN
            portfolio - easier to scan visually for an "is my queue
            balanced?" judgment.
          - Managers: comparative magnitude view (bar) across every case
            - easier to read absolute counts for capacity-planning
            decisions.
        Both reports MUST be present; do NOT consolidate them into
        one shared report. Per AAP Section 0.5.2 cross-file dependencies,
        both reports load AFTER tables/dictionary/choices and BEFORE
        their respective dashboards.

      - Cache discipline: <is_real_time>false</is_real_time>. The
        platform may cache the aggregated counts and refresh on a
        cycle (typically 5 minutes for the Reports module). For a
        POC dashboard with 10 demo cases this distinction is
        cosmetic; for a production case-management deployment with
        thousands of cases it materially affects dashboard load
        time. The default-off setting matches the sibling reports
        (../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d.xml).

      - Chart-size discipline: <chart_size>medium</chart_size>,
        unchanged - though not for want of trying. The measurement
        behind that is recorded here because it bounds what the data
        labels enabled below can show.

        The platform builds a donut data label as "<name> = <value>" and
        clips it to the budget named by
        glide.chart.data.label.truncate_to (13 on this instance) for a
        medium chart, or glide.chart.data.label.truncate_to.large (27)
        for a large one - measured in the platform bundle
        /scripts/GlideV2ChartingIncludes.jsx, where
        hc_addPropsForDataLabelsTruncation() copies the per-report
        budget onto each point and the formatter applies
        label.substring(0, labelMaxLength) + '...'. Five of this
        report's six status labels fit inside 13 characters ("Draft =
        1", "Open = 2", "Closed = 2", "Pending = 1", "Resolved = 2");
        "In Progress = 2" is 15 and is clipped.

        Raising the chart to large is the only lever this application
        owns over that budget, so it was set and measured on the
        rendered dashboard. It made the widget worse rather than better:
        at large the labels stopped painting at all - the <text> nodes
        were emitted with empty content and 0x0 rects, positioned by
        transforms outside the chart box, leaving orphan leader-line
        stubs pointing at nothing, at both 1280 and 375. The platform
        sizes the SVG to the dashboard column while computing label
        geometry for the larger canvas. So the value was reverted to
        medium, where the labels do paint.

        What that leaves: per-slice labels are drawn, and a label longer
        than 13 characters is clipped with an ellipsis. The values
        themselves are not lost - the SVG legend prints each in full and
        <display_grid> below adds a keyboard-reachable (status, count,
        percentage) table. Closing the clipping needs
        glide.chart.data.label.truncate_to raised, which is a global
        sys_properties row and is barred by AAP Section 0.3.2 (no
        global-scope writes): a human with instance administration can
        change that single property and the labels print in full with no
        change to this application.

      - Scope binding: <sys_scope>x_casemgmt</sys_scope> and
        <sys_package source="x_casemgmt">x_casemgmt</sys_package>
        bind THIS record to the same scoped-app row that the parent
        sys_app record (../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml)
        publishes. Cross-file consistency: the sibling reports
        (x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml, x_casemgmt_my_overdue_tasks.xml,
        x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml, x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d.xml)
        use identical scope-reference values, ensuring all reports
        resolve to the same sys_app row at preview time. Reference-
        by-name not reference-by-sys_id per AAP Section 0.7.2
        (no hardcoded sys_ids constraint).

      - <sys_id> determinism: the value
        32ae52ab8735ea76587edee6130abc06 is the lowercase MD5 of the
        canonical record name "x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status". This
        matches the convention documented in
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_my_open_cases.xml lines 219-236
        (md5("x_casemgmt_my_open_cases") = ee04fa982b136ad35c6fe1512826f6ab),
        ../reports/x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close.xml lines 233-247
        (md5("x_casemgmt_avg_time_to_close") = 79fabb5f5754b629205b2382face3ebb),
        and ../reports/x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d.xml lines 71-84
        (md5("x_casemgmt_cases_opened_30d") = 95b2127b026a92ba5e041a8d4d7277ec).
        Deterministic derivation makes the Update Set re-importable
        on a fresh PDI without sys_id collisions: re-importing the
        same XML on the same or any future PDI resolves the same
        sys_id and idempotently updates the same report row rather
        than creating a duplicate. This stability is REQUIRED because
        the Agent Workspace dashboard
        (../dashboards/pa_dashboards_x_casemgmt_agent_workspace.xml)
        references THIS report by `<name>x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status</name>`,
        and the dashboard widget binding must resolve consistently
        across re-imports.

      - Sharing discipline (AAP Section 0.4.4 "agents and managers see
        operationally relevant aggregate views on day one", and AAP
        Section 0.7.3 Gate 6 "accessible to users with correct
        roles"): <roles>x_casemgmt_case_manager,x_casemgmt_case_agent,
        x_casemgmt_case_viewer</roles>. `sys_report.roles` is a
        user_roles column holding role NAMES (no sys_ids, per AAP
        Section 0.7.2), and it is what makes a report reachable by a
        user who is neither its owner nor an admin. With it empty, a
        report is private: an impersonated x_casemgmt_demo_agent
        opening this report received a blank page reading "Report
        visible only to a specific user or group" even though the
        report's filter was correct. All three scoped roles are
        listed because all three personas are legitimate readers of
        aggregate case data - the row-level perimeter is enforced by
        the table ACLs (AAP Section 0.5.6), not by report sharing, so
        an agent still only sees the rows their ACLs permit.
        <user>GLOBAL</user> is the other half of the fix, and the
        load-bearing half: the platform's own read ACL on sys_report is
        a script, and it consults `roles` ONLY on its `isGlobal` branch -
        `isGlobal` is `current.user.toString() == "GLOBAL"`. With `user`
        empty the report is private to whoever created it, the branch
        that reads `roles` never runs, and an impersonated
        x_casemgmt_demo_agent got a blank page reading "Report visible
        only to a specific user or group" even with all three roles
        listed. `sys_report.user` is a 40-character STRING (not a
        reference) whose magic values are 'GLOBAL' (visible to everyone,
        narrowed by `roles`) and 'group' (shared through
        sys_report_users_groups); 265 of 300 sampled out-of-box reports
        carry 'GLOBAL' with `roles` as the restriction, which is exactly
        the shape this report now ships. 'GLOBAL' is a literal platform
        keyword, not a sys_id, so the AAP Section 0.7.2 no-hardcoded-
        sys_id constraint still holds.

