    UPDATE-SET ORDERING
    ===================
    Per AAP Section 0.5.2 (Cross-File Dependencies):
      - AFTER tables, dictionary, choices.
      - AFTER seed-data so that running the report yields non-empty
        results during validation.
      - BEFORE the Agent Workspace dashboard so that dashboard widget
        binding resolves.

    AUTHORING-RULES COMPLIANCE
    ==========================
      - AAP Section 0.7.2 verbatim:
          "No-hardcoded-sys_id constraint. Anywhere - flows, ACL
           conditions, scripts, seed data, UI Policies, UI Actions,
           Business Rules. Every cross-reference is resolved by
           GlideRecord lookup against a stable human-readable key
           (name, user_name, number, role_label)."
        This file: <sys_scope> and <sys_package> resolve by scope
        name; <sys_id> is THIS record's own primary key (not a
        hardcoded reference to another record), deterministically
        derived as MD5(name). <table> resolves by table name. The
        Choice values "Draft", "Open", etc., are NOT references to
        sys_id values - they are Choice labels resolved at render
        time. All compliant.

      - AAP Section 0.7.2 verbatim:
          "No-PII constraint. Synthetic data only."
        This file: report definition contains no PII; the report
        merely AGGREGATES rows from a table. Whatever PII or non-PII
        data is in those rows is the seed-data agent's responsibility
        to keep synthetic. The report definition is PII-free.

      - AAP Section 0.7.2 verbatim:
          "Scope-namespace exclusivity: every artifact ... must live
           in the auto-assigned `x_casemgmt` namespace; zero global-
           scope writes are permitted."
        This file: <name> is `x_casemgmt_case_count_by_status`;
        <table> is `x_casemgmt_case`; <sys_scope> and <sys_package>
        bind to the scoped app. Zero global-scope writes.

      - AAP Section 0.7.2 verbatim:
          "Email-disabled constraint."
        This file: does NOT include any scheduled-email-delivery
        configuration. <is_scheduled>false</is_scheduled>. The only
        consumer is the dashboard widget.

      - AAP Section 0.7.2 verbatim:
          "Single-Update-Set deliverable constraint."
        This file: one of the records that compose the final Update
        Set XML at ../update-set/x_casemgmt_case_management_update_set.xml.

      - AAP Section 0.7.2 verbatim:
          "Minimal-Change Clause. Build only what is specified in the
           user's prompt."
        This file: implements exactly one report row per AAP Section
        0.5.1; no additional fields beyond those mandated by the
        agent prompt; no new dimensions or aggregations beyond
        group-by-status COUNT.

    PERSISTENT SEGMENT LABELS AND A FULL DATA GRID (QA ISSUE 12)
    ============================================================
    QA Issue 12 (MEDIUM, visual/accessibility) measured on the rendered
    Agent Workspace dashboard that this donut carries NO persistent
    segment labels at all - a DOM count of `.highcharts-data-label`
    returned ZERO - so every one of the six status values is reachable
    only by hovering a segment or by reading the legend. At 1280px the
    six-item legend is correct and that is survivable; at 375px the
    legend collapses to a paginated strip reading "1/6", exposing ONE
    value at a time behind non-keyboard paging arrows. The chart is then
    a picture with no readable values on the width most likely to be
    used on a phone.

    ROOT CAUSE, IN THIS RECORD (not inferred)
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    This artifact emitted neither `show_chart_data_label` nor
    `display_grid`. Both are `sys_report` BOOLEAN columns whose
    sys_dictionary default_value is `false`, so an omitted element is not
    "platform default, probably on" - it loads as OFF. Read back from the
    committed row on the instance BEFORE this change:
        show_chart_data_label = false      <- no per-segment labels
        display_grid          = false      <- no fallback data table
        allow_data_label_overlap        = false
        show_data_label_position_middle = false
        show_chart_total                = false
    which is exactly the zero-data-label measurement QA recorded.

    THE IN-SCOPE REMEDY APPLIED
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      - <show_chart_data_label>true</show_chart_data_label>
        Turns on Highcharts data labels for the series, so each of the
        six segments carries its own persistent, on-canvas label instead
        of surrendering its value to a hover or to the legend. The label
        is painted for every segment at every viewport width, so it does
        not depend on the legend surviving the 375px collapse.
        Precedent on this instance: of the 8 `type=donut` reports, 3
        already ship this column enabled ("Today's flow executions by
        state", "On-Call: HoursDistribution by User - 7 days"), so this
        is a supported report option and not a platform modification -
        which keeps the remedy inside AAP Section 0.3.2's prohibition on
        global-scope changes.

      - <display_grid>true</display_grid>
        Renders the report's aggregate as an HTML data table beneath the
        chart, carrying every (status, count, percentage-of-count) row
        plus a total row. This is the SAME stock column the two Manager
        View bar reports already ship for QA finding F8
        (../reports/x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_status.xml,
         ../reports/x_casemgmt_all_cases_by_priority.xml), where it was
        measured to render `table.chart_legend.display-grid-table` with a
        real <thead>, `th scope="col"` column headers, `th scope="row"`
        row headers and a bold total row, outside any
        `aria-hidden="true"` ancestor. It is what makes ALL SIX status
        values reachable at 375px in text, whatever the legend does.
        Precedent is partial and is stated as such rather than
        overclaimed: 6 of the 182 `type=bar` reports on this instance
        enable `display_grid`, but NO `pie` or `donut` report on the
        instance does. `display_grid` is a plain column on `sys_report`
        with no chart-type qualifier in its dictionary row, and the grid
        is emitted by the chart component's legend/grid renderer rather
        than by the series, so it is expected to render here too - but
        that rendering is a RUNTIME fact, and it is owed a browser
        re-verification on the Agent Workspace after the live report row
        is updated. It has not been asserted from this file.

    FIELDS DELIBERATELY LEFT AT false, AND WHY
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
      - allow_data_label_overlap = false (element omitted, dictionary
        default false). Left off ON PURPOSE. With it off, Highcharts
        suppresses a label that would collide with a neighbour rather
        than painting two labels on top of each other; with it on, six
        segments in a 375px-wide widget would produce overlapping,
        unreadable text. The accessible route to a value a label had to
        drop is the data grid enabled above, which carries all six rows
        in text - so enabling overlap would trade a legible chart for a
        cluttered one without adding any value the grid does not already
        carry.
      - show_data_label_position_middle = false (element omitted). Left
        off ON PURPOSE. It moves the label INSIDE the ring; this donut
        renders a 70% centre hole (measured innerR 67.795 against r
        96.85 in the dashboard widget), leaving a ring band far too
        narrow at 375px to hold a status label such as "In Progress"
        without truncation. Off keeps the platform's outside placement
        with connector lines.
      - show_chart_total = false (element omitted). It prints ONE
        aggregate total, which does not address per-segment labelling,
        and it is enabled by zero of the 182 bar reports on this
        instance (measured for QA finding F8).
      - show_legend is NOT set by this record and is deliberately not
        introduced. Measured on the committed row it reads `false`, yet
        the rendered widget still draws the paginating legend QA saw -
        which locates legend rendering, its paging arrows and their
        keyboard reachability in the chart component, not in this
        report. See the residue note below.

    WHAT IS NOT FIXED HERE, STATED AS OPEN
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    The second half of QA Issue 12 - "offering non-keyboard legend
    toggles", and the 375px legend paginating to "1/6" - is NOT closed by
    this record and is not closable from it. The legend, its "1/6" paging
    control and the per-item show/hide toggles are emitted by the
    platform's Highcharts wrapper (GlideV2ChartingIncludes.jsx /
    chart_includes.cssx), a GLOBAL artifact; AAP Section 0.3.2 verbatim
    prohibits "Global scope changes of any kind" and AAP Section 0.7.2
    verbatim requires "zero global-scope writes are permitted". No
    `sys_report` column on this release relocates, resizes or
    keyboard-enables the legend: the record's `legend_*` columns cover
    only background colour, border colour/width/radius, vertical and
    horizontal alignment, left-align and column-align - none of which
    adds a tab stop or a focus ring. The labels and the grid above make
    every value readable WITHOUT the legend, which is the reachability
    the finding asks for; the legend control itself stays OPEN, awaiting
    human authorisation, and is recorded in ../docs/dashboards.md.

    WHAT THIS CHANGE DOES NOT TOUCH
    ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    <filter> is unchanged, byte for byte. It is the per-viewer predicate
    added for QA finding F5 (see "WHY THIS REPORT IS FILTERED" above) and
    widening it would re-open a disclosure. <type>, <field>, <aggregate>,
    <table>, <roles>, <user>, <title> and <chart_size> are likewise
    unchanged: this change adds two accessibility booleans and nothing
    else. <chart_size> was moved to large and measured, because it is
    the only in-scope lever over the data-label truncation budget, and
    was reverted when the labels stopped painting altogether - see
    "Chart-size discipline" above for the measurement and for the one
    global property that would close the clipping.

